# Inventory and Data Safety

Apply these rules to catalog sync, suppliers, purchase orders, receiving, adjustments, stocktakes, transfers, costs, reports, webhooks, workers, exports, and billing data.

## Tenant isolation

- Derive the authenticated shop server-side from Shopify sessions.
- Never trust a client-supplied shop identifier.
- Tenant-scope every read, mutation, relation lookup, unique constraint, background job, export, cache entry, and audit event.
- Validate that referenced IDs belong to the authenticated shop before use.
- Enforce permissions server-side; hiding a control is not authorization.
- Add cross-shop isolation tests for every high-risk action.

## Data authority

- Shopify is authoritative for products, variants, orders, refunds, locations, and sellable inventory.
- App-owned supplier, PO, receipt, cost, operational analytics, and audit records require explicit authority rules.
- Do not claim custom POs automatically create Shopify-native incoming inventory without verified API support.
- Preserve source identifiers, timestamps, snapshots, and reconciliation status.
- Recreated Shopify variants are new identities unless an explicit audited lineage decision says otherwise.

## Inventory-write contract

Every receiving, adjustment, stocktake, cost-sync, and transfer write requires:

- stable operation and idempotency ID;
- authenticated shop and actor;
- permission and location checks;
- intended before/after quantity or cost;
- line-level result;
- bounded retry behavior;
- partial-failure handling;
- immutable audit event;
- reconciliation against Shopify;
- recovery or reversal policy;
- feature or global kill switch;
- tests.

A workflow must not be marked completed when any required Shopify write failed or remains unresolved.

## Operational ledgers

Use immutable events for:

- receipt lines and corrections;
- rejected, extra, cancelled, backordered, and unreceived quantities;
- stock adjustments;
- count submissions and approvals;
- transfer lifecycle changes;
- average and landed-cost changes;
- billing and entitlement changes;
- AI usage and provider cost.

Derived state must be reproducible from source facts or reconciled snapshots.

## Reliability

- Webhook processing must be idempotent and observable.
- Queue retries must be bounded and dead-letter failures visible.
- Use correlation IDs across request, job, Shopify mutation, audit, and reconciliation records.
- Do not hide stale data. Show watermarks and sync-health state.
- Do not use hidden row caps or unpaginated nested GraphQL assumptions.
- Avoid N+1 database and Shopify requests.
- Store timestamps in UTC; apply merchant timezone at reporting and scheduling boundaries.
- Use decimal-safe money and explicit currency handling.

## Migrations and destructive changes

- Prefer additive migrations.
- Describe backfill, rollback, recovery, and tenant impact.
- Do not delete or reinterpret operational history silently.
- Require reconciliation before enabling production writes.
- Protect destructive workflows behind flags and kill switches.